a lot less  moisture in the room to dry out because  you don't have all those stalks and  stems  we also believe that this helps to  reduce the potential for mold growth  just because you have more control and  there's less biomass  um less trichomes taken off the plant  so um in cannabis the thc trichomes that  are on there  you can actually knock them off during a  dry trim and it ends up in like the  byproduct rather than on the flower  so we found here that if you wet trim it  maintains some of those trichomes and  you have a higher thc in your end  product  some things that we have found is that  with a wet trim it takes more cleaning  there's a lot more residue left on the  product when they're done  it takes longer to turn the room over  and we need a lot  more production stuff for a wet trim all  at the same time  so the biggest problem with this is that  we can't we have a big spike in labor  for those days  but we can't sketch anything else on  those days um  we even have to take back like  office staff to help because it's just  too much all at one time  so um we're we've worked through that  now  but it's uh it can be a struggle so  learning  and of course you don't want to bring  back people that don't know  how to handle the product because again  cross-contamination  lots of training involved so we gotta  make sure that we have the right people  doing the right job  okay this is just a little graph of some  of the timing for the initial dry  basically whether you do a dry trim or a  wet trim  you get that same sort of a curve but  we're starting upwards of 70 percent  moisture and we need to get that down  very very quickly  in the first day so um first day  or two days we don't want to have that  higher moisture for too long a time  because then it gives the  mold more time for growth so um  on the other side of that the flip side  if you dry it too quickly  you can get this harsh product so um  a longer cure so basically there's  initial dry  and then there's a curing process that  initial dry  we usually do in about two days we bring  it down to 30  we put it into a closed bucket bag  whatever seal it up for six hours we  take it back out of that bag and that  just  evens out the moisture throughout the  whole lot  put it back on drying racks bring the  moisture down a little bit more  we make sure the available water is less  than about  0.56 and then that makes sure that  we've got minimal potential for mold  growth and then we hold it  at that moisture for another  seven to ten days roughly  now this is the curing so what you see  there is a  sea vault you can use any kind of bucket  to  keep that moisture in um but basically  this is what i was just talking about  that 30 moisture we do this bucket  sweat it doesn't sound very appealing  but that's but what it does it pulls the  moisture out of the  stems in the middle and brings it to the  side so that we can dry it more evenly  we put it back on those drying screens  we bring it under that 0.6  available water and then we bring it  down to around a 12 percent moisture  all right and then we terminology we  burp  the uh the bins every day uh while it's  curing  and that just off-gasses and just make  sure that the product's uh  maintaining a good even moisture  we check the moisture every day we uh  sometimes we'll check terpenes and like  that as well  basically it does we do do a lot of  tests  uh during this period to make sure that  we're monitoring that moisture  and available water as tightly as we can  there's a few things that we can do if  we want to  increase the moisture there's a boveda  pack there there's bavita integra  there's a lot of different types of  packs that  will adjust the moisture to bring it up  or we can spread it out on drying racks  again and bring the moisture down  we use hundreds hundreds of grams  of moisture of flour for moisture tests  so i'll talk to that again in a second  basically we need consistent repeatable  reproducible  um results in order to get those  that quality  all right so this is just nothing to do  with cannabis but we apply food curing  and aging science to cannabis  so that's why we need to understand the  available water and the moisture  we need to understand what is a good  available water and moisture for  cannabis and  another economic trade-off here so 10  moisture product loss and 13 could be  close to mold growth  so we try to keep it in that  height window basically  for every one percent difference in one  batch  it's about a sixteen hundred dollar  difference so if we package our product  at twelve percent versus 11  that's give away right  so this is basically why we cure the  product  just like meat or cheese or tobacco or  anything earring brings out the aromas  and the flavors and it accentuates those  natural terpenes  okay so some of the testing that we do  we use  a rototronic available water meter and  we use  a at53 method toledo moisture analyzer  pretty basic equipment the nice thing  about the available water is that it's a  simple internal test  anybody on the line can do it it's  non-destructive  so it's it's fairly quick it's a good  indicator for mold growth  again taken from the food industry 0.6  seems to be around  where whether it's you know obviously  it's different for different things but  0.6 seems to be where herbs and stuff  like that are good  to avoid mold growth  and it's a really quick test so um  one of the things about that is  especially when the moisture is really  really high  it might only take seven or eight  minutes to get the available water but  if we were to run the moisture test on  it  it might take 45 minutes to an hour  so the moisture analyzer it's also a  simple internal test  we've trained lots of people here how to  do it it's a destructive test  but it is industry standard so um  definitely that's what we use when we're  finally releasing our product  um this is our measurement for giveaway  so  it tells us basically if we're going to  save that sixteen hundred dollars or  thirty two hundred dollars or if we're  going to give it away  um and then it takes much longer as i  was mentioning if the flower is wet or  fresh  so it can take a significantly long time  but the nice thing about it is basically  you  prep it you put it in there you walk  away and you come back for a result so  it's at least not that bad  but we do use hundreds of grams of  product in testing  again just to make sure that we're  monitoring our our moisture  and available water uh which again could  be  hundreds even thousands of dollars  we do take for every batch that we  produce  we do actually have somebody that takes  about eight  hours worth of testing so um just for  moisture  so you know it can add up significantly  at the end of the day all this helps to  ensure a quality product that we can be  proud to put the green seal of approval  on  so just so that we can review quickly um  summary of the trade-offs there's a  significant cost of labor hours  just for cleaning and and maintaining  plant health  but it reduces the chance of mold the  dollar value of the product at 10  versus thirteen percent can be  significantly different so it's uh  value added to do all that extra testing  the cost of testing in-house and  external  adds up but as i just mentioned there's  a reason we do it  um the loss of product due to mold is  significant  so this is why we put so much  preventative  in place um and the consumer perception  so quality of the product  not too dry haste metal harsh product is  uh  very very important  so thank you i appreciate your time  if you do want to reach out to green  seal cannabis our website is there at  the bottom of the page  if you have any specific questions that  you wanted to ask you can send it to  info  greensealcannabis.com or you can send it  directly to me at my email  all right thank you so much again take  care
 

